Key methodologies involve comparing pre- and post-migration wages of the same immigrants to understand the share of income differences attributed to human capital versus country-specific factors, such as physical capital and total factor productivity (TFP). The analysis reveals that upon migrating to the U.S., immigrants experience wage increases that represent 38% of the difference in GDP per worker between their birth countries and the U.S., implying that human capital accounts for around 62% of cross-country income differences. 

